UNIT adopt_replace_block
LANG rust
PRIMARY-SHA256 e6eeba5909802787e54f046addbade224098d3b0b3de51c2fb1aa0cc6d286cb5
SIG pub fn adopt_replace_block(existing: &str, block: (&str, i64, i64), managed: &str, kept: &[&str]) -> String
ABI crate shape: a LIBRARY crate ([lib], src/lib.rs exporting pub fn adopt_replace_block), no dependencies, no main, no
  I/O, no unsafe; the verifier generates the harness from the abstract SIG. text = &str (UTF-8; well-formed by the
  envelope); the record arrives BY VALUE as a tuple in declared field order (heading, startLine, endLine); i64 = i64;
  list[text] = a slice of &str. The result is an owned String. A throw (R2, R3) is a panic; the message is not graded.
  Nothing else panics.
MECHANISM R1 = existing.split('\n') collected (N = count of LF + 1, the empty first and last pieces kept; never
  str::lines, which also cuts at CR LF and drops a final empty line). R2 compares in i64 before any cast to usize (a
  negative or huge value must reach the panic, not wrap). LF and CR are ASCII, so byte offsets at line starts and ends and
  around a CR are char boundaries. WS is NOT char::is_whitespace (that set holds U+0085 and lacks U+FEFF) and NOT
  char::is_ascii_whitespace: test membership in the WS pin explicitly and trim with trim_matches over that predicate (R9).
  std has no General_Category: CF is the explicit CF pin; VISIBLE (R3, R10) is "any char in neither WS nor CF", per char
  (a char is a scalar, so astral CF members are single chars). R5 BLANK is the three chars ' ' '\t' '\r' only. R10's
  tail strip is trim_end_matches(['\r', '\n']). R8 is a hand scan (no regex crate). The HEADER pin is a literal with the
  EM DASH written \u{2014}.
DEPENDS-ON none
PINNED charset WS = '\u{9}' '\u{a}' '\u{b}' '\u{c}' '\u{d}' '\u{20}' '\u{a0}' '\u{1680}' '\u{2000}'..='\u{200a}' '\u{2028}' '\u{2029}' '\u{202f}' '\u{205f}' '\u{3000}' '\u{feff}' CITED R3 R9 — the primary's WS, written as Rust char literals
PINNED charset CF = '\u{ad}' '\u{600}'..='\u{605}' '\u{61c}' '\u{6dd}' '\u{70f}' '\u{890}'..='\u{891}' '\u{8e2}' '\u{180e}' '\u{200b}'..='\u{200f}' '\u{202a}'..='\u{202e}' '\u{2060}'..='\u{2064}' '\u{2066}'..='\u{206f}' '\u{feff}' '\u{fff9}'..='\u{fffb}' '\u{110bd}' '\u{110cd}' '\u{13430}'..='\u{1343f}' '\u{1bca0}'..='\u{1bca3}' '\u{1d173}'..='\u{1d17a}' '\u{e0001}' '\u{e0020}'..='\u{e007f}' CITED R3 R10 — the primary's CF, written as Rust char literals
